-- title:
--   Basis coordinates of elements of I · N lie in I
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $N$ be an additive commutative group carrying an $R$-module structure, and let $\kappa$ be an arbitrary index type. Given a basis $b : \kappa \to N$ of $N$ over $R$ (in the form of a `Module.Basis κ R N`), an ideal $I \subseteq R$, an element $x \in N$, and an index $k : \kappa$, the hypothesis is that $x$ belongs to the submodule $I \cdot \top$ of $N$, that is, the submodule generated by all products $a \cdot n$ with $a \in I$ and $n \in N$ (equivalently $IN$, with $\top$ the whole module $N$). The conclusion is that the $k$-th coordinate $b.\mathrm{repr}\,x\,k \in R$ of $x$ in the basis $b$, i.e. the value at $k$ of the finitely supported coordinate function of $x$, lies in $I$. No finiteness assumption is made on $\kappa$, and the assertion is for the single given index $k$, hence, as $k$ is universally quantified, for every coordinate.
--
--   This is the standard fact that, for a free module $N$ with basis indexed by $\kappa$, an element of $IN$ has all of its basis coordinates in $I$ (so that $IN$ is exactly the set of elements with coordinates in $I$, the converse being immediate). It is used in the treatment of adically continuous Galois representations, where it feeds into [`GaloisRepAdic.continuous_unitsMap_toMatrix_of_isAdicContinuous`](thm.html#GaloisRepAdic.continuous_unitsMap_toMatrix_of_isAdicContinuous), and more broadly in coordinatewise reduction arguments for free modules over patching rings.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Module_Basis_repr_apply_mem_of_mem_ideal_smul_top.lean

